From: Daniil Teterin <theweaklessone@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] leds: htr3212: release the supply across system sleep

On the Odin 2 Portal the two stick-LED controllers share vdd_mcu_3v3 with
the gamepad MCU. rsinput already drops its vote in suspend, but each
controller keeps its own for as long as it is bound, so the rail stayed on
through s2idle with the MCU held in reset behind it.

Release the supply after pulling SDB low, and take it back before raising
SDB on resume. Resume already re-initialises the chip and restores every
LED, so losing the register state costs nothing.

In 25-minute RTC sleeps run in rotated order, unbinding all three consumers
of the rail cut the charge used from 25.9 to 17.5 mAh per cycle (three
cycles each), roughly 70 mW. Unbinding also skips the drivers' resume work,
so the saving from this patch alone may be smaller.
---
--- a/drivers/leds/leds-htr3212.c
+++ b/drivers/leds/leds-htr3212.c
@@ -293,12 +293,25 @@
  * in system sleep with every LED at 0. Pull SDB low for sleep, then bring
  * the chip back up and restore each LED's brightness on resume. No I2C in
  * the suspend path.
+ *
+ * The supply is shared with the gamepad MCU, which only loses power once
+ * nothing else holds the rail, so let go of it here as well. The registers
+ * are lost with it, and resume programs them again anyway.
  */
 static int htr3212_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct htr3212_priv *priv = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int ret;
 
 	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(priv->sdb, 0);
+
+	ret = regulator_disable(priv->vdd);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(dev, "Failed to disable supply: %d\n", ret);
+		gpiod_set_value_cansleep(priv->sdb, 1);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
@@ -308,6 +321,12 @@
 	unsigned int i;
 	int ret;
 
+	ret = regulator_enable(priv->vdd);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(dev, "Failed to enable supply: %d\n", ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+
 	gpiod_set_value_cansleep(priv->sdb, 1);
 	usleep_range(10000, 11000);
 
